  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "yNxzXAJYuTaJatm7",
      "productId": "openmrs",
      "question": "If `UserIcon` in the OpenMRS frontend framework (openmrs-esm-core) changed its behaviour, which code in the patient chart app (openmrs-esm-patient-chart) uses it and could be affected?",
      "generation": "g-b59a21",
      "kind": "grounded",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "# Impact of a `UserIcon` behaviour change on the patient chart",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Short version: `UserIcon` lives in the framework (`esm-styleguide`), and only **two** components in `openmrs-esm-patient-chart` use it, both in the medication ordering/display area, both rendering it as a small (16px) icon inside a tooltip trigger that reveals the name of the person who ordered a medication. Nothing else in that app imports it, and no test mocks it.",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## What `UserIcon` actually is",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "`UserIcon` is one of the framework's generated icon components. It is a memoised `forwardRef` component whose only job is to render the generic `Icon` with the sprite id `omrs-icon-user` [1]. The real behaviour — sizing, fill, the `<svg>` wrapper — sits in `Icon` [2], which defaults `size` to 20 and `fill` to `currentColor` [3], rejects sizes below 1 or above 72 with a console error and falls back to 20 [4], and renders an SVG with `classNames('omrs-icon', style.icon, className)` [5]. The icon path is registered under the id `omrs-icon-user` in the sprite [6], and `UserIcon` reaches apps through `@openmrs/esm-framework`, which re-exports the styleguide public surface [7]. So a change to `UserIcon` means a change to any of: the `IconProps` contract (`className`, `fill`, `size`) [8], the icon id/`use` href, the default size and fill, or the invalid-size guard.",
          "citations": [
            1,
            2,
            3,
            4,
            5,
            6,
            7,
            8
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## The two affected call sites",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Both are local helper components, confusingly both named `InfoTooltip`, but with different signatures and in different packages.",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "**1. Orders app — medication record.** `MedicationRecord` renders `<InfoTooltip orderer={order.orderer?.display ?? '--'} />` next to the start date [9]. That `InfoTooltip` is defined at the bottom of the same file and renders `<UserIcon size={16} />` as the child of a Carbon `ToggletipButton`, with the orderer's name in the `ToggletipContent` [10], imported from the framework on line 5 [11]. `MedicationRecord` is itself used by the orders details table [12] and rendered per matching order inside that table [13]. So the icon appears in the Orders view of the patient chart whenever a rendered medication row shows its orderer.",
          "citations": [
            9,
            10,
            11,
            12,
            13
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**2. Medications app — medications details table.** The `startDate` cell renders `<InfoTooltip orderer={medication.orderer?.person?.display ?? '--'} />`, but only when the table is not in print mode (`!isPrinting`) [14]. Its own `InfoTooltip` renders `<UserIcon size={16} />` inside a Carbon `IconButton` (`kind=\"ghost\"`, `size=\"sm\"`, label = the orderer string) [15]. This one is imported in the big framework import block of the file [16]. Note the behavioural coupling here: print mode deliberately suppresses the icon, so anything that changes the icon's rendered size or box model touches the table layout (and possibly the print layout) of this column.",
          "citations": [
            14,
            15,
            16
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "A good way to read the dependency: the icon is always the *trigger* of a tooltip, never standalone content.",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "```mermaid\n---\ntitle: UserIcon usage in the patient chart\n---\nflowchart TD\n  subgraph fw[\"esm-framework (openmrs-esm-core)\"]\n    ui[\"UserIcon\"] --> ic[\"Icon\"]\n    ic --> sprite[\"omrs-icon-user sprite\"]\n  end\n  subgraph orders[\"esm-patient-orders-app\"]\n    mr[\"MedicationRecord\"] --> mi[\"InfoTooltip\"]\n    mi --> oi[\"UserIcon size 16\"]\n    odt[\"order-details-table\"] --> mr\n  end\n  subgraph meds[\"esm-patient-medications-app\"]\n    mdt[\"medications-details-table\"] --> mt[\"InfoTooltip\"]\n    mt --> mi2[\"UserIcon size 16\"]\n  end\n  ui -.->|\"exported via @openmrs/esm-framework\"| mi\n  ui -.-> mi2\n```",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## Likely, but not verified from the app",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "- `UserIcon` is also mocked in the framework's own test helpers as a plain `<span>UserIcon</span>` [17] and [18]. I did not check whether the patient chart's test setup wires up these mocks, so I cannot say whether the app's tests would notice a change to the real component.\n- Tests that render the affected code: `MedicationRecord` is exercised directly many times in its own test file [19], and the orders-details-table test replaces `MedicationRecord` with a mock altogether [20], meaning a real `UserIcon` change would only be seen by the medication-record tests.",
          "citations": [
            17,
            18,
            19,
            20
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## Name clashes to ignore",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "There is a third `InfoTooltip` in the same repository, in the tests app [21], but it is a local function that renders Carbon's `Information` icon, not `UserIcon`, so it is unaffected. Likewise the `UserAvatarIcon`, `UserFollowIcon` and `UserXrayIcon` components are separate symbols with separate sprite ids [22], and none of them are imported in the patient chart.",
          "citations": [
            21,
            22
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## What I could not check",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Only the two repositories you selected were searched with symbol resolution; other apps (the O3 framework's shared components, `openmrs-esm-core`'s app-shell, or any repository outside this scope) may use `UserIcon` too, and I did not search them. Within the patient chart, the string `UserIcon` appears in exactly the two files above, so an empty result there is not being claimed for anything else. Dynamic dispatch and reflection are not a factor for a React component, but the icon is rendered inside Carbon's `ToggletipButton`/`IconButton`, whose own layout and accessibility behaviour around an SVG child I did not inspect.",
          "citations": []
        }
      ],
